        <!-- Closing CTA -->
        <div class="mt-16 bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-8">
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            <strong class="text-white">A walking pad is worth it if it gets used most days, and not if
            it does not.</strong> Used five days a week for three years, a typical mid-priced pad
            costs well under a dollar a session; used for six weeks and abandoned, the same pad
            costs around ten dollars a walk. The machine is rarely the problem — the plan for using
            it is.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Buy one if you have a slot in the day it will fill, a place it can stay ready, and slow
            walking is what you want. Look elsewhere if you want to run, want incline, or are hoping
            the purchase will supply the motivation. If it is a yes, our
            <a href="/walking-pad-buying-guide/" class="text-[#5AA9FF] font-bold no-underline">walking pad buying guide</a>
            covers how to choose one that lasts.
          </p>
